Yes, this specific topology and hardware imbalance will heavily impact your failover performance, especially given your high-throughput, write-heavy (Kafka) and read-heavy workload.

Your current architecture layout consists of:

Node 1: Arbiter (0-data, vote only)

Node 2: Primary (High configuration)

Node 3: Secondary (High configuration)

Node 4: Secondary with sync source as a secondary (Low configuration) — This introduces Cascading Replication.

Why This Configuration Causes Major Post-Failover Lag
1. The Impact of Cascading Replication (Node 4 syncing from Node 3)
By default, MongoDB automatically chooses a sync source for secondaries. If Node 4 is explicitly or dynamically syncing from Node 3 (the main secondary) instead of the Primary, Node 3 has to shoulder a double burden:

It must fetch and apply oplogs from the Primary (Node 2).

It must simultaneously serve outgoing oplog data to Node 4 over the network, plus handle your heavy application reads.
This turns Node 3 into a major networking and CPU bottleneck.

2. The Danger of Failing Over to Node 3 (The Secondary)
Because Node 2 (Primary) and Node 3 (Secondary) have high configurations, you might think failing over to Node 3 is safe. However:

If Node 3 was already bogged down processing heavy reads and serving Node 4's cascading replication, its WiredTiger cache and CPU will be heavily constrained.

The moment Node 3 is promoted to Primary, it must instantly flip from consuming replication to accepting heavy write traffic from all your Kafka connectors, processing writes, and pushing oplogs outward. If its resources are already saturated, application jobs will instantly lag.

3. The Danger of Failing Over to Node 4 (Low Configuration)
If an election happens and Node 4 (the low-configuration node) gets elected or takes over traffic:

It lacks the CPU, RAM, and disk IOPS to handle heavy Kafka writes.

The WiredTiger cache will instantly overflow, lock contention will skyrocket, Flow Control will aggressively throttle your application, and the entire database pipeline will grind to a crawl.

Note: Node 4 has a low configuration and should ideally never become primary.

How to Investigate Your Current Setup
Check Current Sync Sources:
Run the following command on your secondary nodes to see who is syncing from whom:

JavaScript
db.adminCommand({ replSetGetStatus: 1 })
Look at the syncSourceHost field for each member to verify if Node 4 is depending on Node 3.

Monitor Replication Latency Chain:
Check how far behind Node 3 is from the Primary, and how far behind Node 4 is from Node 3 using rs.printSecondaryReplicationInfo(). If Node 4 shows high lag, cascading replication is failing to keep pace.

How to Fix and Optimize the Architecture
Eliminate Cascading Replication (Force Direct Sync):
Configure your replica set members to pull directly from the Primary rather than chaining through another secondary. You can influence or restrict sync targets using replica set member configurations if necessary, ensuring all secondaries sync straight from the primary source of truth.

Isolate Read/Write Priorities (priority and votes):
To prevent your low-configuration node (Node 4) from ever accidentally becoming a Primary during a flaky network event or failover, explicitly set its priority to 0:

JavaScript
// Run in primary shell to adjust node 4 priority to 0
cfg = rs.conf();
cfg.members[3].priority = 0; // Assuming node 4 is index 3
rs.reconfig(cfg);
This ensures Node 4 can still vote (if needed) and hold a copy of the data, but it can never be elected primary.

Match Hardware Configurations:
For high-throughput time-series and Kafka workloads, having asymmetric node configurations creates a single point of failure and bottleneck. Upgrading Node 4 to match Nodes 2 and 3—or removing it if it's redundant—will drastically stabilize your post-failover recovery times.
